## When you need the commercial licence

You need it if either applies:

* **You offer swadesh, or a modified version of it, as a network service** and are unwilling or
  unable to publish the source of that service under the AGPL (AGPL section 13).
* **You ship swadesh inside a product distributed in binary or object form** — including an
  application, an appliance or an SDK — and do not wish to release that product under the AGPL.

## When you do not need it

* Running swadesh inside your own organisation to score your own data. This is free under the
  AGPL and needs no commercial licence.
* Using swadesh as a build- or evaluation-time tool whose output you own, where swadesh itself is
  not distributed to your users.
* Publishing your work under the AGPL, or an AGPL-compatible licence.

If you are unsure which side of a line you are on, ask before you ship.

